WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.300
Abbey Road! The Beatles' famous crosswalk --

00:00:02.300 --> 00:00:05.100
or zebra crossing, as we'd call it in Britain,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.540
because of the black and white stripes

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:09.990
and the weird thing we have about naming our
crossings after creatures.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:13.880
Now, jaywalking -- crossing the road where
there isn't a marked crossing --

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.940
isn't illegal in most of Britain.

00:00:15.940 --> 00:00:18.710
We can cross wherever it's safe. Which is
just as well,

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:21.210
given the complicated layout of our cities,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:24.189
and given how often day trippers recreating
that album cover

00:00:24.189 --> 00:00:28.399
hold up traffic here on what is actually a
fairly busy London road.

00:00:28.399 --> 00:00:30.519
But crossing the road safely,
wherever you are,

00:00:30.519 --> 00:00:34.780
requires both the people driving and the people
walking to take care.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.329
Yes, a driver not paying attention could kill
someone --

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:42.129
but someone on foot could walk out into traffic
without looking,

00:00:42.129 --> 00:00:45.030
assuming they're safe because,
hey, it's a crosswalk,

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.320
and even the best driver with brand new brakes

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:49.719
has a limit on how quickly they can stop.

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:53.010
And there's plenty of research to say that,
in some situations,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:56.480
marked crosswalks like this one
are actually less safe.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.879
The US Department of Transportation analysed
five years of data from 1,000 marked crosswalks,

00:01:01.879 --> 00:01:04.860
and 1,000 matching sites that had no markings.

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.780
On roads like this? The markings made no difference.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.659
On bigger, multi-lane roads?

00:01:10.659 --> 00:01:13.670
A marked crosswalk is more dangerous,

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.399
probably because if a car stops in the first
lane,

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.000
pedestrians don't always have a sightline

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.490
to see any cars still speeding past in the second

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.049
-- and the cars can't see them either.

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:25.780
Does that mean we should remove
all the road markings

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.320
and let everyone fend for themselves?

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.310
Well, we can work it out.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:30.979
There are "shared space" schemes,

00:01:30.979 --> 00:01:32.509
based on studies from the Netherlands,

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:34.969
like this one in Exhibition Road in London.

00:01:34.969 --> 00:01:38.020
The theory is because there aren't strict
road markings,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.600
everyone is more cautious, cars go a bit slower,

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:44.439
and people look both ways. Great in theory,

00:01:44.439 --> 00:01:47.049
maybe not so good if you're
blind or partially sighted.

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:50.469
The debate about whether this is a good thing
is still going on.

00:01:50.469 --> 00:01:52.780
And ultimately, the only way to be sure,

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:55.689
is for pedestrians to look when
they're crossing the road --

00:01:55.689 --> 00:01:59.429
and drivers to assume that people on foot
aren't paying attention to you.

00:01:59.429 --> 00:02:01.409
'Cos at Abbey Road?

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:02.219
They mostly aren't.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.500
